Z||000000||LottaBallsCamaro||10-09-2001||04:27 PM||||Got a coupla questions if yall dont hate me to much. <P>1. Will the 3800 out of the camaros work in a fiero?<P>2. What kind of 1/4mi. times will a 200hp fiero put out?<P>3. How much would you expect to spend on a 3.4 TDC?<P>4. Does the 3.4TDC have an alluminum block?<P>5. What kind of skidpad and slalom numbers will a 88'GT put out?<P>6. Where can I find a rebuild kit for an 88'?<P>7. How much will it cost me to lower one?<P>8. Is there a rear sway bar on the 88's<P>9. If not, how much will that cost me?<P>10.Did any fiero's come with Ttops<P>Sorry this post is so long, but my nissan 4*4 just cost me $300 to fix after I took it mudding, so I am probably looking to sell it and I cant decide if I want to buy another camaro or a fiero. The camaro is probably going to cost me more to do what I want to it. Also, I dont see the point in sinking money in two basically the same camaro's when the first one isnt at the point of finished that I want it to be. If you dont like me dont flame me please. <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Spektrum-87GT||10-09-2001||05:14 PM||||well, i dont know what you did earlier in this forums, because im fairly new... but you live close, so i'll be nice.. the answer you're going to get from near EVERYONE is go for the fiero! everyone and their brothers uncle owns a camaro or mustang, be orginal! i'll answer the following questions the best of my knowledge, but most are rough estimates and things i've read off the net.<P>1. dont know, i asked earlier and no one answered<P>2. low 15s?<P>3. used one just went on ebay, complete for 660, expect to pay 1000 more for the swap and i doubt you'll get that kinda price. do a little research on the tdc first, cheaper of the swaps, but EXPENSIVE rebuild if need be.<P>4. dont know, a lot of forged parts, i know that<P>5. 87 got a .7 something i think(some random statistic) not sure about the 88<P>6. rebuild a 3.1 stroker! they go on ebay for 500... but thats just for the stroker kit(crankshaft, pistons, piston rods, more)<P>7. well... the right way or ghetto way? i've seen 2" lowering kits for 300 bucks, but you can always cut them<P>8. 88 gt, yes, not sure about the coupe or formula.. pretty sure there is one in the formula<P>9. not much<P>10. 88's did, expect to pay a lot, because unlike mienekie, you're not going to get a lot, but you're going to pay a lot, at least for a 88 gt. Most years(every?) had a sunroof option and the sunroof is HUGE, its almost like a removable hard top!<P>i attempted to answer the questions, because it seems like you made a few forum members mad before... might be the only response  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><P>------------------<BR>87 fiero gt under semi restoration/modification. progress at <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/spektrum_87gt/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/spektrum_87gt/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-09-2001||05:33 PM||||OK.  I'm all for giving you a second chance.<P>Here are the answers I can help you with.<P>1.  No idea but I doubt it.<P>2.  Once again, no idea but keep in mind that the Fiero weighs less than 3000# so whatever time you could get out of a Camaro with the same engine will be alot more than the same setup in a Fiero.<P>3.  About 4 grand, installed.<P>4.  I don't think so, but there are more than one variation, so one of them might.<P>5.  Don't know this one, either.<P>6.  What do you want to rebuild?  Motor?  Suspension?<P>7.  It'll cost a hell of a lot more to lower an '88 than it will to lower the rest.  If you really want to lower a Fiero, do yourself a favor and buy a pre-'88.  You'll end up with the same handling for less money.  You can have a heck of a suspension for well under a grand.<P>8.  Only on the GTs and Formulas.<P>9.  You can buy aftermarket sway bars for less than $150 for the rest of the years/models.<P>10. T tops were a dealer option on the '88s (at least on the GTs, possibly others).  Some of the '86s and '87s had "aftermarket" T tops all (or at least, most) were installed by the same company that did the dealer installed '88s (C&C).||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gerald||10-09-2001||07:46 PM||||The rest of the answers you got were right to the best of my knowledge. But you didnt get a number on the skid pad so last i saw was mid .8s. Keep in mind that a mclaren F1 only pulled off 8.4. A Gt fiero is right around that number ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Wannabe||10-09-2001||09:53 PM||||1. Conventional wisdom says no, because the starter is on the wrong side, but some resourceful person will probably find a way to make it work.<BR>2. Mid to high 14's? Some guys are running low 15's with the stock 2.8 and some bolt on mods (not nitrous).<BR>3. $1000-$1500 for engine, harness and ecm<BR>4.No.<BR>5. 0.84 was the number published by Motor Trend. This was on the GT+4 all season tires.  Also, the '88 had a slightly softer suspension that earlier GT's and so had slightly less grip potential, although better overall handling.<BR>6. Rebuild for what part of the car?<BR>7. ??<BR>8. GT's and Formulas have a rear sway bar.<BR>9. To add a rear bar to a coupe, you'd need the bar and the mounting brackets. I've seen them offered used for as low as $150 for everything. Not that easy to find. You can also purchase new mounting brackets from Held Motorsports for $??.<BR>10. Yes.||||reg||1
Z||000005||LottaBallsCamaro||10-09-2001||11:01 PM||||I'm sorry about the rebuild thing, I meant the suspension(bushings,ball joints, tierods)and such. A fiero doesnt have the potential to go as fast in a straight line as a camaro or mustang, because of the transmission, I've never heard of a GM FWD trans taking 600hp. Also there is the factor of the limitation on rear wheel wideness as long as the compared cars dont have the amount of power that make traction and trans strength a factor then yes. Hey Specktrum, How old are you? My girlfriend goes to CNU, Where do you live?  <p>[This message has been edited by LottaBallsCamaro (edited 10-09-2001).]||||reg||1
